    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nTwo structural issues, the first clear-cut:\n\n**1. The new method was inserted between `formatContactPreference`'s Javadoc and its method, orphaning the doc.** Look at lines 316\u2013352:\n- Lines 316\u2013323 are the Javadoc describing `contactPreference` (\"Derives an owner's preferred contact channel\u2026 `'EMAIL'`\u2026 otherwise `'PHONE'`\").\n- The new `formatOwnerSegment` Javadoc (324\u2013334) and method (335\u2013344) were spliced in *right after* that block.\n- Result: the `contactPreference` Javadoc now floats above `formatOwnerSegment` (which has two Javadoc blocks stacked on it), and `formatContactPreference` at line 346 is left with no Javadoc at all.\n\nEvery other `@Named` formatter in this interface is a single Javadoc block immediately followed by its method. The new method should be added as its own self-contained unit (e.g. after `formatContactPreference`, or before it but *above* the `contactPreference` Javadoc), not wedged between an existing method and its documentation. A maintainer scanning this file would be confused about which doc belongs to which method.\n\n**2. AREA is derived by calling the timezone lookup as a proxy for \"known region\", which is a misleading coupling.** Line 342:\n```java\nString area = LocalityResolver.timezone(formatLocality(owner)) != null ? \"METRO\" : \"REGIONAL\";\n```\nThe spec defines AREA by whether the locality is a *known region* (NSW/VIC/QLD) \u2014 a membership question that has nothing to do with timezones. This works today only because `REGION_TIMEZONE` happens to list exactly the same three regions, but it expresses region-membership by computing an IANA timezone name and discarding it. A reader asks \"why are we resolving a timezone to pick a segment area?\", and anyone later editing the `REGION_TIMEZONE` table would silently change segmentation.\n\n`formatLocality` already returns the sentinel `\"UNKNOWN\"` precisely when no region resolves, so the intent is expressed directly and in the locality's own vocabulary with:\n```java\nString area = !\"UNKNOWN\".equals(formatLocality(owner)) ? \"METRO\" : \"REGIONAL\";\n```\nAlternatively, if region-membership is worth naming, add an explicit `isKnownRegion(region)` to `LocalityResolver` (which owns the region knowledge) and call that. Either keeps the \"known region\" concept in the right unit instead of borrowing the timezone table's null behavior.",
